          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which factor in this patient's history is most directly responsible for the development of her vesicovaginal fistula?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Delivery of a macerated stillborn baby</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fetal death was a consequence of the prolonged obstructed labour, not the cause of the fistula. The fistula resulted from sustained tissue compression during labour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Prolonged obstructed labour lasting nearly two days</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Obstetric VVF results from prolonged obstructed labour, where the fetal head compresses the vesicovaginal septum against the bony pelvis, causing ischaemic necrosis and subsequent fistula formation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Home delivery without skilled birth attendance</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Absence of skilled care prevented timely intervention such as caesarean section, but the fistula itself was caused by the mechanical compression of tissues during the obstructed labour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Maternal malnutrition and poor general health</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Malnutrition may impair tissue healing after injury, but the vesicovaginal fistula was directly caused by ischaemic pressure necrosis from the prolonged obstructed labour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the case: the direct mechanism is prolonged obstructed labour causing ischaemic necrosis of the vesicovaginal septum. Other factors are contributory, not causative."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Prolonged obstructed labour is the single most important cause of obstetric VVF in developing countries — and a preventable one."
                }
              ],
