          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What is the primary rationale for the syndromic approach to STI management recom</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What is the primary rationale for the syndromic approach to STI management recommended by NACO and the WHO?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It eliminates the need for laboratory investigations entirely</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The syndromic approach does not eliminate the need for laboratory tests — it allows treatment to begin before results are available, preventing loss to follow-up in settings where labs are delayed or unavailable.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It allows treatment at the first clinical encounter in resource-limited settings</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The syndromic approach was designed for settings where laboratory diagnosis is unavailable, delayed, or costly — enabling immediate treatment at the first encounter and dramatically reducing ongoing community transmission.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It is more accurate than pathogen-specific laboratory diagnosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Laboratory diagnosis is more accurate for identifying specific pathogens. The syndromic approach prioritises timely treatment over pathogen-specific diagnosis to reduce transmission.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It reduces antibiotic resistance by using narrower-spectrum regimens</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Syndromic management often uses broad-spectrum empirical regimens. Its advantage lies in reducing community transmission through prompt treatment, not in narrowing antibiotic spectra.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key insight is that syndromic management was designed for resource-limited settings where waiting for lab results means patients are lost to follow-up and continue transmitting infection."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Syndromic management ensures no patient leaves untreated simply because a lab result is unavailable — a critical strategy in resource-limited settings across India."
                }
              ],
