          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 22-year-old woman presents with her first episode of PID caused by ascending chlamydial infection. Which statement best justifies the urgency of completing treatment and ensuring partner management?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The risk of tubal-factor infertility is negligible after a single treated PID episode</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Even a single PID episode carries a 15–20% risk of tubal-factor infertility. While prompt treatment reduces further damage, the risk from even one episode is clinically significant.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tubal damage from PID is cumulative and largely irreversible, with infertility risk exceeding 50% after three episodes</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. PID causes cumulative, largely irreversible tubal scarring — 15–20% infertility risk after one episode, rising to over 50% after three. This justifies urgent treatment and partner management to prevent reinfection and further episodes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Partner treatment is necessary only when the partner has symptomatic urethritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Partners must be treated regardless of symptoms — asymptomatic carriage of chlamydia and gonorrhoea is common. Untreated partners cause reinfection, negating the patient's treatment.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ascending chlamydial infection primarily damages the ovarian parenchyma rather than the fallopian tubes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ascending chlamydial infection primarily damages the fallopian tubes and endometrium, causing tubal scarring, occlusion, and a 7-fold increased risk of ectopic pregnancy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key justification is cumulative harm: tubal damage from PID is largely irreversible, and infertility risk escalates dramatically with each episode — from 15–20% after one to over 50% after three."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well reasoned. The cumulative and irreversible nature of tubal damage is what makes every PID episode consequential and every reinfection preventable through partner management."
                }
              ],
